---
import { Card } from "@origami-systems/ui";
import MainLayout from "~/layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout
    title="Privacy Policy - Origami Systems"
    description="How Origami Systems collects, uses, shares, and protects personal information."
>
    <article class="blog">
        <header>
            <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
            <div class="description">
                Effective date: November 23, 2026 · Last updated: November 23,
                2026
            </div>
        </header>
        <hr />
        <Card class="summary-card">
            <div class="summary">
                <h2>The short version</h2>
                <p class="summary-note">
                    This summary is here to help you understand the policy
                    quickly. It does not replace the full Privacy Policy below.
                    The summary and the full policy are not separate agreements.
                    When you accept, you are accepting the full policy, and if
                    anything in this summary differs from the full text, the
                    full text controls.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        We collect what we need to run the apps. That includes
                        your account details, what you do in the apps, and basic
                        technical information such as your IP address and
                        browser.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        We do not sell your information, show you ads, or track
                        you across other websites.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        If a school or organization manages your account, it
                        controls that account and can see your activity in its
                        apps.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        On a standard sign-up form, anyone who can open the form
                        can see the names of the people who signed up. Only
                        organizers see email addresses. Anonymous forms hide
                        names from everyone except organizers.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        A few companies help us run the Services, such as Stripe
                        for payments, Google for email, and Sentry for finding
                        bugs. Sentry may record how pages behave, with all text
                        and typing hidden.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        You can delete a personal account in the Account app. It
                        is permanently deleted 7 days later, and you can change
                        your mind during those 7 days.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        You must be 13 or older to create a personal account.
                        Younger children can use accounts their school sets up.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        Questions or requests go to <a
                            class="text-link"
                            href="mailto:privacy@origamisystems.org"
                            >privacy@origamisystems.org</a
                        >.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </Card>
        <div class="content">
            <p>
                Origami Systems LLC ("Origami Systems," "we," "us," or "our")
                builds and runs software applications, websites, and related
                services.
            </p>
            <p>
                This Privacy Policy explains what personal information we
                collect, how we use, store, protect, and share it, and the
                choices available to individuals, parents, guardians, and
                organizations.
            </p>
            <p>
                This policy covers these Origami Systems products and services:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>The House App.</strong> Manages house systems, points,
                    games, events, announcements, and participation for an organization.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>The Signups App.</strong> Lets people create sign-up forms,
                    collect registrations, and manage participants and waitlists.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Origami Systems Identity.</strong> Our sign-in and account
                    service. It provides accounts, authentication, and account settings
                    for the other Services.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Other Origami Systems services.</strong> Our websites,
                    the Account app, onboarding, help and support pages, administrative
                    dashboards, notification services, and any other application that
                    links to this policy.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                We call these the "Services." Not every Service collects
                everything described below. What we collect depends on which
                Services and features you use, your account type, and your
                organization's settings. A Service may give you an additional
                privacy notice when its features require one.
            </p>
            <h2 id="information-we-collect">Information we collect</h2>
            <p>
                We collect the information we need to provide, maintain, secure,
                and improve the Services.
            </p>
            <h3>Account and identity information</h3>
            <p>
                When you create or use an account through Origami Systems
                Identity, we may collect:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    First, middle, and last name, display name, name prefix, and
                    username
                </li>
                <li>Email addresses and whether they are verified</li>
                <li>
                    Date of birth, when an organization or feature requires it
                </li>
                <li>
                    Account type (personal, organization-managed, or guest),
                    status, and preferences
                </li>
                <li>Organization memberships and roles</li>
                <li>When the account was created and when it last signed in</li>
                <li>Sign-in methods and credential records</li>
                <li>Information needed to recover or verify the account</li>
                <li>
                    The version of our Terms of Use and this Privacy Policy you
                    accepted, and when you accepted it
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                We never store plain-text passwords. We store passwords as
                one-way cryptographic hashes. We encrypt email addresses and
                authenticator secrets when we store them.
            </p>
            <p>
                Sign-in records can include passkeys and security keys,
                authenticator-app (TOTP) settings, backup codes, and
                passwordless sign-in settings.
            </p>
            <h3>External and organization sign-in</h3>
            <p>
                You may be able to sign in with Google or Microsoft. An
                organization may also connect its own identity provider using
                single sign-on (SAML or OpenID Connect), or keep its member list
                in sync with ours through SCIM directory provisioning.
            </p>
            <p>
                When you use one of these methods, the provider sends us the
                information needed to identify you and set up your account and
                session, such as your name, email address, and a provider
                account identifier. When an organization uses directory
                provisioning, its system may create, update, or deactivate your
                account.
            </p>
            <p>
                We never receive your password from Google, Microsoft, or your
                organization's identity provider.
            </p>
            <h3>Authentication and security information</h3>
            <p>To run and protect the Services, we collect:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Sign-in sessions and session identifiers</li>
                <li>
                    Sign-in times, including successful and failed attempts and
                    the reason an attempt failed
                </li>
                <li>IP addresses</li>
                <li>Browser and device information, such as the user agent</li>
                <li>
                    Security events, rate-limit records, and multi-factor
                    authentication status
                </li>
                <li>Account recovery and credential changes</li>
                <li>Administrative and security audit records</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                We use this information to sign people in, block unauthorized
                access, investigate security incidents, and keep the Services
                reliable.
            </p>
            <h3>Organization information</h3>
            <p>Organizations provide or manage information such as:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Organization names and identifiers</li>
                <li>Members, roles, permissions, and account assignments</li>
                <li>Administrators and authorized contacts</li>
                <li>
                    Organization settings, profile policies, and enabled
                    applications
                </li>
                <li>Single sign-on and directory provisioning settings</li>
                <li>Administrative activity</li>
                <li>Subscription and billing information</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                An organization administrator may add information about members
                or create accounts for them.
            </p>
            <h3>Application information</h3>
            <h4>The House App</h4>
            <p>The House App may collect and process:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Organization, house, and year information</li>
                <li>House assignments and memberships</li>
                <li>Points, the reasons points were awarded, and standings</li>
                <li>Games, brackets, activities, and participation records</li>
                <li>Events and calendars</li>
                <li>Announcements, comments, and other submitted content</li>
                <li>Archived years and participation history</li>
                <li>Administrative actions and audit records</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                The House App also supports guests invited by an administrator.
                For guests we may collect:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Guest names or generated display names</li>
                <li>Guest-group and house assignments</li>
                <li>Invitations, invitation use, and expiration dates</li>
                <li>Guest participation in games and activities</li>
                <li>Guest comments and notification preferences</li>
                <li>
                    Identifiers stored in the browser that let a guest return to
                    their guest account
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Other members of the organization may see this information,
                depending on their roles and permissions.
            </p>
            <h4>The Signups App</h4>
            <p>The Signups App may collect and process:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Form titles, descriptions, colors, settings, and access
                    codes
                </li>
                <li>Event names, locations, dates, and times</li>
                <li>Sections, slots, and capacity</li>
                <li>
                    Participant names, email addresses, and account identifiers
                </li>
                <li>
                    Registration status, cancellations, and waitlist position
                </li>
                <li>Private cancellation links for guest registrations</li>
                <li>
                    Notification preferences for registration confirmations and
                    cancellations
                </li>
                <li>Form ownership and administrative activity</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Some forms let people register as a guest without an Origami
                Systems account. A guest registration includes a name and email
                address.
            </p>
            <p>Who can see a registration depends on the form's settings:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Standard forms.</strong> Anyone who can open the form
                    can see the names of the people signed up for each slot. Only
                    the form's creator and other authorized organizers can see participant
                    email addresses.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Anonymous forms.</strong> Other participants and visitors
                    cannot see who signed up. The form's creator and authorized organizers
                    can still see every registration, including names and email addresses.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                A form creator can also require an access code, which limits who
                can open the form.
            </p>
            <p>
                Form creators decide what their forms collect and are
                responsible for making sure their collection and use of that
                information follows the law.
            </p>
            <h4>Origami Systems Identity</h4>
            <p>
                Origami Systems Identity provides sign-in and account management
                for the other Services. It processes the account,
                authentication, and security information described above.
            </p>
            <p>
                Identity shares limited account information with other Origami
                Systems applications when they need it to sign you in, check
                your permissions, manage your account, or provide a feature you
                asked for.
            </p>
            <h4>Other services</h4>
            <p>
                Other Origami Systems applications may collect information they
                need for their own features. When their practices differ from
                what this policy describes, we will update this policy or give a
                separate notice.
            </p>
            <h3>Support and contact information</h3>
            <p>
                When you contact us, fill out a contact form, or submit a bug
                report, we may collect:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Your name and email address</li>
                <li>Your organization or company name</li>
                <li>How you heard about us</li>
                <li>The content of your message or report</li>
                <li>
                    Information we need to look into and resolve your request
                </li>
                <li>Related messages and support records</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Billing and subscription information</h3>
            <p>
                When an organization or individual buys a subscription, we may
                collect or process:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Billing names, addresses, and contact information</li>
                <li>Organization billing information</li>
                <li>Subscription plans, status, and billing history</li>
                <li>Transaction identifiers and payment status</li>
                <li>Information needed for invoicing, accounting, and taxes</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Stripe processes our payments. You enter payment card details in
                a secure payment form provided by Stripe, and the card number
                goes directly to Stripe. We do not receive or store full card
                numbers. We keep Stripe customer and subscription identifiers
                and the billing records described above. Stripe handles card
                information under its own privacy policy.
            </p>
            <h3>Device, diagnostic, and technical information</h3>
            <p>When you use our websites or applications, we may collect:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>IP addresses</li>
                <li>Browser type and version</li>
                <li>Device and operating system information</li>
                <li>Request and error logs</li>
                <li>Diagnostic and performance information</li>
                <li>Push notification subscriptions</li>
                <li>Application preferences</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                We use Sentry to find and fix errors. In some of our web
                applications, Sentry also records a sample of sessions (session
                replay) so we can see how a page behaved when something went
                wrong. Session replay masks all text and form input and blocks
                images and media before the recording leaves your browser, so
                the recordings show page structure, clicks, and navigation, not
                what you typed or read. We have configured Sentry not to collect
                user identity information, cookies, request headers, request
                bodies, or URL query parameters.
            </p>
            <h2 id="where-information-comes-from">
                Where information comes from
            </h2>
            <p>We get information from:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>You.</strong> When you create an account, register for
                    something, submit content, or contact us.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Organization administrators.</strong> When an organization
                    creates or manages accounts, memberships, settings, or participation
                    records.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Other users.</strong> When an authorized user adds your
                    information, such as a form creator or administrator managing
                    participants.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Your device and browser.</strong> When you use the Services
                    and technical information is generated.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Sign-in providers.</strong> When you sign in with Google,
                    Microsoft, or your organization's identity provider, or when your
                    organization syncs its directory with us.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Service providers.</strong> When they send us information
                    we need for billing, security, support, or operations.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                We process this information only for the purposes described in
                this policy or as the law otherwise permits.
            </p>
            <h2 id="personal-and-organization-managed-accounts">
                Personal and organization-managed accounts
            </h2>
            <h3>Personal accounts</h3>
            <p>
                You can create a personal account to use supported Origami
                Systems applications. One personal account works across multiple
                applications. Origami Systems manages the information needed to
                sign you in and keep your account secure.
            </p>
            <h3>Organization-managed accounts</h3>
            <p>
                Organizations can create and manage accounts for their members.
                Depending on its settings, an organization's administrators can
                control:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Account creation and membership</li>
                <li>Which applications a member can use</li>
                <li>Roles and permissions</li>
                <li>
                    Profile information, including whether members can change
                    their own name or birthday
                </li>
                <li>Participation records and assignments</li>
                <li>Suspending or removing an account</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                If you have an organization-managed account, you may need to ask
                your organization's administrator to make some changes or to get
                information the organization controls.
            </p>
            <h3>Who is responsible for the information</h3>
            <p>
                Organizations decide what information goes into the Services for
                their activities and how they use it. For that information,
                Origami Systems processes personal information on the
                organization's behalf, under our agreements with the
                organization and the law.
            </p>
            <p>
                For other information, such as what we need to run account
                services, protect our systems, or meet our own legal
                obligations, Origami Systems decides how and why it is
                processed.
            </p>
            <p>
                Which party is responsible depends on the information, the
                Service, and the law that applies.
            </p>
            <h2 id="how-we-use-information">How we use information</h2>
            <p>We use information to:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Provide and operate the Services</li>
                <li>Create, sign in, and manage accounts</li>
                <li>
                    Manage organizations, memberships, roles, and permissions
                </li>
                <li>
                    Process registrations, waitlists, and participation records
                </li>
                <li>
                    Provide points, standings, games, calendars, and other
                    features
                </li>
                <li>
                    Send account, organization, registration, and application
                    notifications
                </li>
                <li>Give administrators management and reporting tools</li>
                <li>Process subscriptions and billing</li>
                <li>Respond to support requests and messages</li>
                <li>
                    Detect, investigate, and prevent fraud, abuse, and
                    unauthorized access
                </li>
                <li>
                    Find and fix errors and improve reliability and performance
                </li>
                <li>Enforce our agreements and meet legal obligations</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                <strong
                    >We do not sell personal information. We do not use personal
                    information for targeted advertising, and we do not build
                    advertising profiles.</strong>
            </p>
            <p>
                We do not use information collected from students through
                school-authorized use for advertising or any commercial purpose
                unrelated to the Service the school requested.
            </p>
            <h2 id="information-shared-between-origami-systems-services">
                Information shared between Origami Systems services
            </h2>
            <p>
                Origami Systems applications share infrastructure for sign-in,
                accounts, organizations, notifications, and security. When you
                use more than one application, each one can access the limited
                account information it needs to:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Identify and sign in your account</li>
                <li>Confirm your organization membership</li>
                <li>Check your permissions</li>
                <li>Provide the features you use</li>
                <li>Send notifications</li>
                <li>Keep your account secure</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                For example, an application may receive your account identifier,
                display name, or organization membership from Origami Systems
                Identity.
            </p>
            <p>
                Information you create in one application does not automatically
                appear in the others. Access depends on the application, your
                permissions, and your organization's settings.
            </p>
            <p>
                We do not disclose one organization's information to another
                organization because both use Origami Systems.
            </p>
            <h2 id="how-we-share-information">How we share information</h2>
            <p>
                We share information only to provide the Services, run our
                business, comply with the law, or as this policy otherwise
                describes.
            </p>
            <h3>Within organizations</h3>
            <p>
                Authorized users in the same organization may see information
                about each other. Depending on their permissions, administrators
                can view or manage:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Account and membership information</li>
                <li>Roles and permissions</li>
                <li>House or group assignments</li>
                <li>Participation and registration records</li>
                <li>Announcements, comments, and submitted content</li>
                <li>Administrative and audit information</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Other members may see standings, activities, events, or comments
                when those features are available to them.
            </p>
            <h3>With form creators and organizers</h3>
            <p>
                When you register through The Signups App, the form's creator
                and authorized organizers receive what they need to manage your
                registration. That includes your name, email address, selected
                slots, and registration status. <a
                    class="text-link"
                    href="#information-we-collect">Section 1</a
                > explains what other visitors to a form can see.
            </p>
            <h3>With service providers</h3>
            <p>
                We use service providers to help run the Services. They process
                information only to provide their services to us, under their
                contracts with us and their legal obligations. They include:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong
                        >Cloud hosting, database, and file storage providers</strong
                    >, which host our applications and store our data
                </li>
                <li><strong>Stripe</strong>, for payment processing</li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Google</strong>, which sends our email through
                    Google Workspace
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Sentry</strong>, for error reporting, performance
                    monitoring, and session replay
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Google and Microsoft</strong>, when you choose to
                    sign in with them
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Browser and device push services</strong>, such as
                    those run by Google, Apple, and Mozilla, which deliver push
                    notifications you turn on
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>A workflow automation tool we operate</strong>,
                    which routes contact-form messages to our support inbox
                </li>
            </ul>
            <h3>For legal and safety reasons</h3>
            <p>
                We may disclose information when we reasonably believe it is
                necessary to:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Comply with the law or valid legal process</li>
                <li>
                    Protect the rights, safety, or security of users or others
                </li>
                <li>Investigate fraud, abuse, or security incidents</li>
                <li>Enforce our agreements</li>
                <li>Protect the Services</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Business transfers</h3>
            <p>
                If Origami Systems LLC is part of a merger, acquisition,
                financing, reorganization, or sale of assets, information may
                transfer as part of that transaction. We will give notice when
                the law requires it, and the information will remain subject to
                this policy unless you are notified otherwise.
            </p>
            <h2 id="cookies-browser-storage-and-similar-technologies">
                Cookies, browser storage, and similar technologies
            </h2>
            <p>
                We use cookies, browser storage, and application memory to keep
                you signed in, remember your preferences, and support guest
                access. We do not use them for advertising or cross-site
                tracking.
            </p>
            <h3>Sign-in cookies and tokens</h3>
            <p>
                When you sign in on the web, Origami Systems Identity sets
                secure, HttpOnly cookies on its own domain to hold your session
                and refresh token. Scripts on the page cannot read these
                cookies. They last up to 14 days, after which you need to sign
                in again.
            </p>
            <p>
                Applications keep short-lived access tokens in memory only.
                Access tokens expire after 15 minutes, and the application gets
                a new one in the background while your session is active.
            </p>
            <p>
                When you sign out, we end the session on our servers and clear
                the sign-in data the application stored in your browser.
            </p>
            <h3>Guest access</h3>
            <p>
                In The House App, guest sign-in information, including the
                guest's tokens, organization, and expiration date, is stored in
                the browser's local storage. This lets a guest return to the
                same guest account on that browser until the guest access
                expires.
            </p>
            <p>
                In The Signups App, guests get a private cancellation link after
                registering. Anyone with the link can cancel that registration,
                so keep it private.
            </p>
            <h3>Preferences</h3>
            <p>
                We use local storage to remember settings such as your color
                theme, your most recent organization, and the sign-in method you
                chose.
            </p>
            <h3>Push notifications</h3>
            <p>
                When you turn on push notifications, your browser or device
                gives us a push subscription, which we use to deliver
                notifications. You can turn push notifications off in your
                account settings or in your browser or device settings.
            </p>
            <h3>Managing browser storage</h3>
            <p>
                You can clear cookies and local storage in your browser
                settings. Doing so may sign you out, remove access to a guest
                account, or reset your preferences.
            </p>
            <h2 id="data-retention">Data retention</h2>
            <p>
                We keep personal information only as long as we reasonably need
                it to provide the Services, fulfill the purposes in this policy,
                meet legal obligations, resolve disputes, and maintain security.
                Different information has different retention periods.
            </p>
            <h3>Account information</h3>
            <p>
                We keep account information while the account is active. <a
                    class="text-link"
                    href="#data-deletion-and-account-closure">Section 9</a
                > explains what happens when a personal account is deleted. Information
                tied to organization-managed accounts and organization activity may
                stay under the organization's control.
            </p>
            <p>
                We delete personal account registrations that were started but
                never completed after 7 days.
            </p>
            <h3>Organization and application information</h3>
            <p>
                Organization records, participation history, sign-up forms,
                registrations, and other application content remain until:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>An authorized user or administrator deletes them</li>
                <li>The organization asks us to delete them</li>
                <li>Our agreement with the organization ends</li>
                <li>The information is no longer needed for the Service</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                We may keep some information longer when the law, an agreement,
                or a security need requires it.
            </p>
            <h3>Guest information</h3>
            <p>
                We keep guest information until the guest access expires, an
                administrator removes it, the guest group or invitation is
                invalidated, or we no longer need it. Some records may remain
                for a short time for security, legal, or backup reasons.
            </p>
            <h3>Authentication and security records</h3>
            <p>
                Origami Systems Identity keeps authentication records for these
                periods:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Successful sign-in records: up to 180 days</li>
                <li>Failed sign-in records: up to 90 days</li>
                <li>
                    Records of revoked sessions, including sessions you signed
                    out of: up to 180 days after revocation
                </li>
                <li>
                    Password reset links: deleted 14 days after they are used or
                    expire
                </li>
                <li>Sign-in sessions: expire after 14 days</li>
                <li>Access tokens: expire after 15 minutes</li>
                <li>
                    Temporary sign-in codes and authorization states: expire
                    within minutes to a day, depending on their purpose
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                An automated cleanup process removes expired records every 12
                hours. We may keep specific records longer for an active
                security investigation or when the law requires it.
            </p>
            <h3>Application and infrastructure logs</h3>
            <p>
                We keep technical logs to monitor performance, fix problems, and
                investigate security incidents. These logs are generally deleted
                after 90 days, unless we need them longer for an active security
                investigation or the law requires it.
            </p>
            <h3>Billing information</h3>
            <p>
                We keep subscription, billing, and transaction records for as
                long as tax, accounting, and other laws require.
            </p>
            <h3>Support records</h3>
            <p>
                We keep contact and support records as long as we need them to
                answer your request, keep reasonable business records, and
                handle related issues.
            </p>
            <h3>Backups</h3>
            <p>
                We keep encrypted database backups for disaster recovery.
                Backups are generally deleted after 30 days. Deleted information
                may remain in a backup until that backup expires. We do not use
                backups for normal operations, and we may keep a backup longer
                only when the law or an active security investigation requires
                it.
            </p>
            <h2 id="data-deletion-and-account-closure">
                Data deletion and account closure
            </h2>
            <h3>Personal accounts</h3>
            <p>
                You can delete your personal account in the Account app, or by
                emailing <a
                    class="text-link"
                    href="mailto:privacy@origamisystems.org"
                    >privacy@origamisystems.org</a
                >.
            </p>
            <p>
                When you ask to delete your account, we disable it in every
                application right away and schedule it for permanent deletion 7
                days later. During those 7 days you can sign in to the Account
                app and cancel. After 7 days we permanently delete the account,
                its sessions, its sign-in records, and its credentials.
            </p>
            <p>
                If you are the only administrator of an organization, you need
                to add another administrator or close the organization before
                you can delete your account.
            </p>
            <p>
                Deleting your personal account removes your access to every
                Origami Systems application that uses it.
            </p>
            <h3>What happens to your application data</h3>
            <p>
                <strong>The Signups App.</strong> We delete forms you created, along
                with their sections, slots, and registrations. Your registrations
                on other people's forms no longer show your name or email address
                and appear as "Deleted user." Registrations for slots that have not
                ended yet are canceled, which frees the spot for someone on the waitlist.
                Registrations for past slots stay as anonymous history so organizers
                keep accurate records.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Organization content.</strong> Content you contributed to
                an organization, such as announcements, uploaded files, and audit
                records, stays with the organization but is no longer linked to your
                account.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Other Services.</strong> We delete, de-identify, or keep other
                information according to the law, our agreements, and the type of
                information.
            </p>
            <h3>Organization-managed and invited accounts</h3>
            <p>
                Some Services, including The House App, use accounts that an
                organization creates or invites you to. The organization manages
                these accounts, and you cannot delete them yourself in account
                settings.
            </p>
            <p>
                Authorized administrators can deactivate, archive, or remove
                these accounts. The organization may keep participation records,
                such as points, standings, and activity history, as the law and
                its agreements allow.
            </p>
            <p>
                You, or your parent or guardian, can still ask us for access to
                or deletion of personal information when the law gives you that
                right. We may handle the request together with the organization.
            </p>
            <h3>Organization data</h3>
            <p>
                An organization can ask us to delete its information or end its
                use of the Services. We may keep certain records when the law,
                security, or another lawful purpose requires it.
            </p>
            <h3>Privacy requests</h3>
            <p>
                For help with deleting an account or with your personal
                information, email <a
                    class="text-link"
                    href="mailto:privacy@origamisystems.org"
                    >privacy@origamisystems.org</a
                >. We will respond as the law and our agreements require.
            </p>
            <h2 id="your-choices-and-privacy-rights">
                Your choices and privacy rights
            </h2>
            <p>Depending on where you live, you may have the right to:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Access your personal information</li>
                <li>Correct inaccurate information</li>
                <li>Delete your personal information</li>
                <li>Get a copy of certain information</li>
                <li>Object to or restrict certain processing</li>
                <li>Withdraw consent when we rely on it</li>
                <li>Appeal our decision on your request</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                To make a request, email <a
                    class="text-link"
                    href="mailto:privacy@origamisystems.org"
                    >privacy@origamisystems.org</a
                >.
            </p>
            <p>
                If an organization controls the information, we may send your
                request to that organization or work with it to respond.
            </p>
            <p>
                We may ask for information to confirm your identity and your
                authority to make the request. We will respond within the time
                the law requires. We may keep or decline to delete some
                information when the law permits or requires it, such as for
                security, legal compliance, or resolving disputes.
            </p>
            <h3>Account and notification settings</h3>
            <p>
                You can update your profile, sign-in methods, and notification
                preferences in your account settings, unless your organization
                manages those settings. You can also turn off push notifications
                in your browser or device settings.
            </p>
            <h2 id="children-and-students">Children and students</h2>
            <p>
                Schools and other organizations that work with minors may use
                some Services. We limit the collection and use of children's
                personal information to what the Service needs.
            </p>
            <h3>School-authorized use</h3>
            <p>
                Schools may use the Services to manage student accounts,
                participation, activities, and other school functions.
            </p>
            <p>
                Where the law allows, a school may consent on behalf of parents
                to the collection of personal information from children under 13
                for a school purpose. We rely on that consent only when the
                school has authority to give it and the required notices have
                been provided.
            </p>
            <p>
                We use information from school-authorized use only to provide
                the Service the school requested and related functions the
                school authorized. We do not use it for targeted advertising or
                unrelated commercial purposes.
            </p>
            <h3>Parent and guardian rights</h3>
            <p>
                Parents, guardians, and authorized schools can ask to review or
                delete a child's personal information, or ask us to stop
                collecting or using it, as the law allows. Send these requests
                to the organization's administrator or to <a
                    class="text-link"
                    href="mailto:privacy@origamisystems.org"
                    >privacy@origamisystems.org</a
                >. We may confirm the requester's identity and authority before
                acting.
            </p>
            <h3>Personal accounts and other organizations</h3>
            <p>
                You must be at least 13 years old to create a personal Origami
                Systems account. Other age or consent rules may apply where you
                live.
            </p>
            <p>
                Children under 13 may use accounts provided by their school for
                school purposes, subject to the required notices and consent.
            </p>
            <p>
                An organization that is not a school cannot consent for a
                child's parent or guardian. When the law requires parental
                consent, the organization must obtain it before adding the
                child's personal information.
            </p>
            <p>
                We do not require a child to give more personal information than
                the Service needs.
            </p>
            <h3>Public sign-up forms</h3>
            <p>
                Anyone can open a public sign-up form, and some forms allow
                registration without an account. Form creators decide what their
                forms are for and what they ask.
            </p>
            <p>
                A parent, guardian, or authorized organization may register a
                child, as the law allows. Children's privacy laws still apply to
                public forms. We do not require age verification to view a
                public form.
            </p>
            <p>
                If you believe we collected personal information from a child
                under 13 without the required consent, email <a
                    class="text-link"
                    href="mailto:privacy@origamisystems.org"
                    >privacy@origamisystems.org</a
                > and we will delete it.
            </p>
            <h2 id="data-security">Data security</h2>
            <p>
                We use administrative, technical, and physical safeguards to
                protect personal information, including:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Encryption of data sent over networks</li>
                <li>Password hashing and other credential protection</li>
                <li>
                    Encryption of email addresses, authenticator secrets, and
                    other sensitive values in storage
                </li>
                <li>
                    Multi-factor authentication, passkeys, and security keys
                </li>
                <li>Role-based permissions</li>
                <li>Rate limits on sign-in and account emails</li>
                <li>Security logging and monitoring</li>
                <li>Restricted access to servers and databases</li>
                <li>Encrypted backups and recovery procedures</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                No method of transmission or storage is completely secure. If a
                security incident affects your personal information, we will
                investigate it and notify you or your organization when the law
                or our agreements require it.
            </p>
            <h2 id="where-we-process-and-store-information">
                Where we process and store information
            </h2>
            <p>
                We process and store information in the United States. Some
                service providers may process information in other countries to
                provide their services. When the law requires safeguards for
                international transfers, we put them in place.
            </p>
            <h2 id="third-party-services-and-websites">
                Third-party services and websites
            </h2>
            <p>
                The Services may link to other websites or connect with outside
                services, such as Stripe, Google, Microsoft, your organization's
                identity provider, or websites that users and organizations link
                to. Those services have their own privacy policies, which apply
                when you use them directly.
            </p>
            <h2 id="changes-to-this-policy">Changes to this policy</h2>
            <p>
                We may update this policy when our Services, practices, or legal
                obligations change. When we do, we will update the "Last
                updated" date above. If a change materially affects how we
                collect, use, or share personal information, we will give
                additional notice through our websites, applications, email, or
                your organization, and ask for your consent when the law
                requires it.
            </p>
            <h2 id="contact-us">Contact us</h2>
            <p>
                If you have questions about this policy or your privacy rights,
                contact:
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Origami Systems LLC</strong><br />Privacy requests: <a
                    class="text-link"
                    href="mailto:privacy@origamisystems.org"
                    >privacy@origamisystems.org</a
                ><br />General support: <a
                    class="text-link"
                    href="mailto:support@origamisystems.org"
                    >support@origamisystems.org</a
                ><br />Security support: <a
                    class="text-link"
                    href="mailto:security@origamisystems.org"
                    >security@origamisystems.org</a
                ><br />Website: <a
                    class="text-link"
                    href="https://origamisystems.org"
                    >https://origamisystems.org</a>
            </p>
            <p>
                For questions about an organization-managed account or
                information an organization controls, you can also contact your
                organization's administrator.
            </p>
        </div>
    </article>
</MainLayout>

